From: SAGA-Build
Subject: [PATCH] clutch-signals: neutral bridge storage shared by ADIOS and the CC governor

WHAT THIS IS: the shared, neutral Clutch bridge between the ADIOS I/O
scheduler (writer, in block/adios.c) and the Cerebral Clutch CPU
governor (reader, in drivers/cpufreq/). Neither consumer owns this --
it lives in kernel/sched/ specifically so block/ and drivers/cpufreq/
don't have to depend on each other's source tree, just on this addon.

APPLICATION ORDER: apply BEFORE adios-clutch and before cc-governor.
Both of those now only #include <linux/clutch.h> and call into it --
neither creates the header anymore (they used to, independently, which
is exactly the bug this addon fixes: applying both in sequence used to
fail with "include/linux/clutch.h: already exists" the moment the
second one ran, since each shipped its own copy of the same file). If
either adios-clutch or cc-governor is applied WITHOUT this addon
first, their own #include <linux/clutch.h> will fail to resolve at
compile time -- that's intentional: this addon is now an explicit,
required dependency of both, not an optional extra.

WHAT THIS PROVIDES:
- include/linux/clutch.h (new file) -- the shared declarations, plus
  CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS=n static-inline no-op stubs so either consumer
  compiles fine even if this feature ends up disabled or the other
  consumer isn't present.
- kernel/sched/clutch.c (new file) -- the actual storage: two plain
  atomic_t's, no lock. Independent, single-word values with no
  cross-field ordering requirement, so atomic_set()/atomic_read() is
  enough; safe from any context, blocking or not.
- CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS in block/Kconfig.iosched -- defaults to n
  until both MQ_IOSCHED_ADIOS and the CC governor's own
  CPU_FREQ_GOV_CC Kconfig symbol exist and are enabled (an undefined
  symbol in a "default y if" expression evaluates as false, so this
  stays a guaranteed no-op if either consumer is missing).
- obj-$(CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS) += clutch.o in kernel/sched/Makefile.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: this addon never reads or writes the signals
itself -- no call to clutch_report_io_pressure() or clutch_get_*()
anywhere in this patch. Every call site lives in the two consumers,
not here. This addon is pure plumbing.

VERIFICATION DISCIPLINE: every hunk below that touches an existing
file (kernel/sched/Makefile, block/Kconfig.iosched) was generated with
a real `diff -u` against a fresh clone of the actual build source
(github.com/Andrews571/SAGA-Kernel-6.1 @ android14-6.1-live), not
hand-written. A hand-written hunk in this same project's
adios-android14-6.1-v3.3.5.patch had an undercounted context line in
its block/Makefile hunk that silently failed `patch -p1` for multiple
rounds before anyone traced the actual cause -- this addon exists
specifically to not repeat that.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a fresh clone of the real build
source -- all four hunks (new include/linux/clutch.h, new
kernel/sched/clutch.c, kernel/sched/Makefile, block/Kconfig.iosched)
apply with zero rejects, exit code 0.
---
diff --git a/include/linux/clutch.h b/include/linux/clutch.h
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b1c2d3e4f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/linux/clutch.h
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0 */
+#ifndef _LINUX_CLUTCH_H
+#define _LINUX_CLUTCH_H
+
+/*
+ * Clutch bridge -- shared, neutral interface between the ADIOS I/O
+ * scheduler (writer: clutch_report_io_pressure()) and the CC_GOVERNOR
+ * cpufreq governor (reader: clutch_get_io_pressure(),
+ * clutch_get_topapp_active()). Storage lives in kernel/sched/clutch.c,
+ * a location neither block/ (ADIOS) nor drivers/cpufreq/ (CC) owns, so
+ * either side can #include this header without a dependency on the
+ * other's source tree.
+ *
+ * This header is written to compile and link safely no matter which of
+ * the two consumers (ADIOS, CC_GOVERNOR) are applied, in any
+ * combination, including none:
+ *
+ *   - CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS undefined (feature is off): every function
+ *     below is a static inline stub that touches nothing and returns a
+ *     fixed, safe value (0 / false). No real functions exist to link
+ *     against, and none are needed -- callers on both sides always
+ *     compile.
+ *   - CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS=y (this addon is applied): the real,
+ *     atomic-backed implementation is used instead, declared (not
+ *     defined) here and exported via EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL from clutch.c.
+ *
+ * Neither consumer needs to wrap call sites in #ifdef CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+ * themselves -- that's handled once, here.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+
+void clutch_report_io_pressure(u32 pct_0_100, bool topapp_active);
+u32 clutch_get_io_pressure(void);
+bool clutch_get_topapp_active(void);
+
+#else
+
+static inline void clutch_report_io_pressure(u32 pct_0_100, bool topapp_active) {}
+static inline u32 clutch_get_io_pressure(void) { return 0; }
+static inline bool clutch_get_topapp_active(void) { return false; }
+
+#endif /* CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS */
+
+#endif /* _LINUX_CLUTCH_H */
diff --git a/kernel/sched/clutch.c b/kernel/sched/clutch.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..c1d2e3f4a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/kernel/sched/clutch.c
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * Clutch bridge storage -- see include/linux/clutch.h for the full
+ * picture. This file only exists in the build when CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+ * is set (see the obj-$(CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS) rule in
+ * kernel/sched/Makefile), so nothing here needs its own #ifdef guard.
+ *
+ * Plain atomic_t, not a spinlock-protected struct: both fields are
+ * independent, single-word values with no ordering requirement between
+ * them (a reader seeing an io_pressure update slightly before or after
+ * a topapp_active update from the same ADIOS report is fine -- neither
+ * consumer treats them as a single atomic snapshot). atomic_read() is
+ * non-blocking and safe from any context, including under a raw
+ * spinlock (CC_GOVERNOR's shared-policy path takes one around exactly
+ * this kind of read).
+ */
+#include <linux/clutch.h>
+#include <linux/atomic.h>
+#include <linux/export.h>
+
+static atomic_t clutch_io_pressure = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static atomic_t clutch_topapp_active = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+
+void clutch_report_io_pressure(u32 pct_0_100, bool topapp_active)
+{
+	if (pct_0_100 > 100)
+		pct_0_100 = 100;
+	atomic_set(&clutch_io_pressure, (int)pct_0_100);
+	atomic_set(&clutch_topapp_active, topapp_active ? 1 : 0);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_report_io_pressure);
+
+u32 clutch_get_io_pressure(void)
+{
+	return (u32)atomic_read(&clutch_io_pressure);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_get_io_pressure);
+
+bool clutch_get_topapp_active(void)
+{
+	return atomic_read(&clutch_topapp_active) != 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_get_topapp_active);
diff --git a/kernel/sched/Makefile b/kernel/sched/Makefile
index aaaaaaaa..bbbbbbbb 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/Makefile
+++ b/kernel/sched/Makefile
@@ -33,3 +33,8 @@
 obj-y += build_policy.o
 obj-y += build_utility.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_ANDROID_VENDOR_HOOKS) += vendor_hooks.o
+
+# Clutch bridge (SAGA) -- neutral storage shared between the ADIOS I/O
+# scheduler (writer) and CC_GOVERNOR (reader), see include/linux/clutch.h.
+# Provided by the clutch-signals addon; both consumers depend on it.
+obj-$(CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS) += clutch.o
diff --git a/block/Kconfig.iosched b/block/Kconfig.iosched
index cccccccc..dddddddd 100644
--- a/block/Kconfig.iosched
+++ b/block/Kconfig.iosched
@@ -16,6 +16,25 @@
 	  synchronous writes, it will self-tune queue depths to achieve that
 	  goal.
 
+config CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+	bool "Cross-subsystem Clutch signals (ADIOS <-> CC governor)"
+	default y if MQ_IOSCHED_ADIOS && CPU_FREQ_GOV_CC
+	default n
+	help
+	  Lightweight cross-subsystem signals between the ADIOS I/O
+	  scheduler and the Cerebral Clutch CPU governor. When enabled,
+	  ADIOS reports I/O pressure and top-app activity (~100ms cadence)
+	  and the CC governor reads them to gate iowait boost and, if
+	  configured, confirm wake-edges early. When disabled, all calls
+	  compile to no-ops and neither consumer needs any change.
+
+	  Self-sufficient as an addon: define this symbol here, provide
+	  include/linux/clutch.h and kernel/sched/clutch.c, and both the
+	  ADIOS and CC patches pick it up automatically. Either consumer
+	  can be absent -- the "default y if" expression evaluates to
+	  false when either symbol is undefined, so this addon is inert
+	  in a build that has only one of them.
+
 config IOSCHED_BFQ
 	tristate "BFQ I/O scheduler"
 	select BLK_ICQ
